  and an exclusive economic zone of 200  nautical miles  china argues that the paracel and  spratly islands are part of its  territory  and therefore they own anything within  those exclusive economic zones  be it fish or natural resources and it's  been enforcing this in increasingly  hostile ways  china's general military philosophy is  something called  active defense and the idea is to appear  to be non-threatening and to be  defensive  but to create situations where you lure  the opponent into making a move which  then allows you to respond in  force and in  leave fully for you well therefore  the possibility for all the consequences  so what it's doing now is using these  islands as a means of  uh projecting power so it's successfully  intimidated vietnam and stopped it  developing its offshore  oil and gas reserves it's done the same  thing to the philippines  it's tried to do the same thing with  malaysia but with less success  [ __ ] effect has established a kind  of  a block on the other countries  developing their maritime resources i  suppose the next thing that might happen  will be that then china might  try and use its force to develop  resources which doesn't have any claim  to under the u.n convention  or the sea in 2016 an international  tribunal in the hague  ruled that the paracel and spratleys  were rocks rather than islands  and therefore would not get the benefits  of eez  the ruling also stated that there was no  legal basis for china's claims and what  that meant was that  suddenly the value of the disputed  islands became much  less in theory because it meant you  couldn't claim  huge amounts of resources oil and gas  and fish from around them  however china has refused to recognize  this ruling  and the reason is obvious is that it  actually  diminishes the value of these disputed  islands  and makes the the case for the  surrounding countries vietnam malaysia  philippines brunei  much much stronger for the resources for  the oil and the gas and the fish  diplomats in china and asean countries  have turned their attention to an  earlier legally binding document in the  hope of a resolution  the asean nations and china said to have  reached an agreement to begin  negotiations on a code of conduct in the  south china sea  unfortunately the long-term outlook is  not good the fact is that you have  a negotiation process between asean and  china  that is not really going very well  they're trying to establish  a code of conduct that would set some  parameters for how business is conducted  in south china sea  at the same time however they don't  actually address the dispute  at all so no dispute resolution  mechanisms are really being negotiated  seriously at this time  what's perhaps more concerning is the  ever-evolving  china u.s competition dynamic which is  becoming more aggressive in nature  the u.s is response in the south china  sea has been mainly  driven uh from its sort of overall  strategic interests  because the south china sea connects  america's west coast  from california to the indian ocean and  to the middle east and so they said it's  a vital  strategic corridor for them you know for  civilian shipping but also military  shipping to go through  during the obama administration uh the  issue was very contentious already  and so when trump came in what really  happened was not that the  the conflict arose but the way that the  u.s government began to pursue  its strategy became much more aggressive  became much more proactive and  abrasive that's why the situation in the  south china sea has become much more  dangerous  a u.s presence has been increasing in  the region since 2012.  over the years administrations have  weighed in on arguments over resources  but haven't taken sides in the  territorial disputes  until now beijing doesn't respect the  fundamental democratic values  or those enshrined in the asean charter  previous years  we've seen a lot of positive language  from  regional partners who wish to make room  for china as a global  superpower but china's coming a little  hard  the u.s has issued one of its strongest  warnings against  china over beijing's illegal territorial  claims  in the south china sea it's not china's  maritime empire  the united states is prepared to do the  things  necessary to assist them in protecting  their valid legal claims  [Music]  expansion of the chinese navy has become  one of the latest concerns for u.s and  asean nations  beijing's anti-ship ballistic missiles  are also known as carrier killers  a department of defense report suggests  that china may be the first ever to  attach this category of weapon to a  warship  there's a good reason to think that a  lot of china's activities in the  south china sea in terms of building up  its bases are done  with a possible invasion of taiwan in  mind  they're thinking if there was ever an  invasion an attempt to forcibly  you know bring taiwan into the people's  republic of china  that the united states would try and  defend taiwan and having these islands  there with missiles already on them and  runways and so forth would be a way of  keeping the americans at bay it's also i  think part of  trying to create a a bastion a hiding  place for their  ballistic missile submarines there and a  way of just keeping the americans away  from their southern coast  both china and the us regard the south  china sea as pretty  crucial to their overall strategic  interests which means they're going to  struggle hard  the escalation of military activities in  the south china sea  even in the midst of a pandemic has many  onlookers concerned  a war of words on twitter and state tv  has transformed into warships and  military drills  that could be the start of a new cold  war  in the final waning days of the trump  administration  you've seen a slew of measures taken and  i think a lot of those measures  have been taken to try and prevent joe  biden's administration from coming in  and undoing a lot of what president  trump had put into place during his time  in office  based on what we've heard from the biden  administration  it does seem that the strategy will be  to work more closely with allies if the  biden administration is able to form a  united front  as it comes to the south china sea i  think two things could happen  uh if it becomes a an alliance against  china i think you will see the chinese  become more aggressive and pushing back  on that  the alternative would be some sort of  more inclusive uh  alliance that wants to welcome china  into the fold and if that is something  that happens i think you can see  a much more benign resolution to the  problem  [Music]  obviously there are a lot of things  going on in the united states i don't  think the south china sea is  particularly high on joe biden's  priority list at the moment  but i think in the future in the next  couple of years that is what will start  to develop  [Applause]  you

